WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.513
To determine the output width, count valid window starting positions.

00:00:05.513 --> 00:00:15.031
For an input of width 7 and a filter of width 3, with no padding and stride 1, a zero-indexed window can start at 0 through 4.

00:00:15.031 --> 00:00:17.297
That gives 5 output positions.

00:00:17.297 --> 00:00:22.963
The same reasoning applies to height, so a square input produces a 5x5 map.

00:00:22.963 --> 00:00:30.140
This derivation is safer than memorizing a formula without understanding what each term counts.

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:34.323
Stride is the distance between successive window starts.

00:00:34.323 --> 00:00:38.566
At stride 2, starts 0, 2 and 4 fit, giving 3 outputs per axis.

00:00:38.566 --> 00:00:41.440
At stride 3, starts 0 and 3 fit, giving 2.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.972
A start at 6 would overrun the image.

00:00:43.972 --> 00:00:50.611
The lecture describes this last setting as not fitting exactly and shows a fractional expression.

00:00:50.611 --> 00:00:59.850
In standard valid convolution, we keep the complete windows and skip the remaining border, so the size is the floor of that expression.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:07.730
The general formula is the floor of (input size + 2 * symmetric padding - filter size) / stride + 1.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:13.603
It assumes dilation 1 and a padded input large enough for at least one window.

00:01:13.603 --> 00:01:16.691
Width and height are computed separately.

00:01:16.691 --> 00:01:30.018
Some APIs called 'same' padding choose asymmetric padding for certain strides, so use their documented convention rather than assuming it matches this symmetric-padding formula.

00:01:30.018 --> 00:01:35.440
Our calculator follows the explicit symmetric convention in the lecture.

00:01:36.233 --> 00:01:45.052
Without padding, each convolution can shrink the activation map because the filter must remain entirely within the image.

00:01:45.052 --> 00:01:48.987
Padding adds values around the border, commonly zeros.

00:01:48.987 --> 00:01:53.433
With padding 1, a 7-wide input becomes a 9-wide padded array.

00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:59.992
A 3-wide filter at stride 1 then has 7 valid starts, preserving the original spatial size.

00:01:59.992 --> 00:02:09.393
The border outputs combine real values with inserted zeros, so they do not have exactly the same information as interior outputs.

00:02:09.743 --> 00:02:15.795
For an odd filter width at stride 1, padding (filter width - 1) / 2 preserves the width.

00:02:15.795 --> 00:02:22.810
A 3-wide filter needs padding 1, a 5-wide filter needs padding 2, and a 7-wide filter needs padding 3.

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:26.317
The lecture uses these settings as common examples.

00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:39.383
This rule preserves tensor dimensions; it does not reverse the filter's smoothing or feature-detection effect, and it does not imply that every input pixel contributes equally to the result.

00:02:39.733 --> 00:02:43.636
Padding is a hyperparameter choice, not a trainable weight.

00:02:43.636 --> 00:02:48.597
It changes which patches the layer visits and can affect boundary behavior.

00:02:48.597 --> 00:02:53.823
Symmetric zero padding also differs from reflecting or repeating border values.

00:02:53.823 --> 00:03:00.041
When implementing the layer, ensure the forward pass and its gradient use the same convention.

00:03:00.041 --> 00:03:05.795
In an architecture table, record input size, filter width, stride and padding together.

00:03:05.795 --> 00:03:12.013
Saying only that a layer uses a 3x3 kernel is insufficient to determine its output dimensions.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.695
Now reproduce the lecture's numerical example.

00:03:17.695 --> 00:03:19.929
The input is 32x32x3.

00:03:19.929 --> 00:03:26.846
We have 10 filters with spatial size 5x5, stride 1 and padding 2.

00:03:26.846 --> 00:03:30.996
Each filter spans all 3 input channels.

00:03:30.996 --> 00:03:35.146
First calculate the spatial dimensions.

00:03:35.146 --> 00:03:37.912
(32 + 4 - 5) / 1 + 1 = 32.

00:03:37.912 --> 00:03:45.680
10 filters supply the output depth, so the activation volume is 32x32x10.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:48.935
Count the learned numbers separately.

00:03:48.935 --> 00:03:52.546
One filter has 5 * 5 * 3 weights, which is 75.

00:03:52.546 --> 00:03:57.413
Under our convention it also has 1 bias, giving 76 parameters.

00:03:57.413 --> 00:04:00.632
10 filters give 760 trainable parameters.

00:04:00.632 --> 00:04:04.792
The output contains 10,240 activations for one image.

00:04:04.792 --> 00:04:11.701
Reusing the filter across those positions creates more activations, not more parameters.

00:04:11.701 --> 00:04:17.510
Confusing these counts hides the main efficiency gained by weight sharing.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:25.133
If we change only the stride to 2, spatial size becomes 16 while the filter bank remains unchanged.

00:04:25.133 --> 00:04:28.879
The output is 16x16x10, still using 760 parameters.

00:04:28.879 --> 00:04:35.417
If we change only the number of filters to 20, depth doubles and parameter count doubles.

00:04:35.417 --> 00:04:41.588
If the input channel count changes, each ordinary filter's weight count changes too.

00:04:41.588 --> 00:04:48.420
Always identify which architectural quantity was changed before deciding what stays constant.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:53.440
Our executable example makes the dot product visible.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:59.200
A 5x5 single-channel image contains a small repeated cross-like pattern.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:06.400
With an all-one 3x3 filter, zero bias, no padding and stride 1, the first patch sums to 4.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:07.920
The next sums to 5.

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:14.720
The complete 3x3 result has center value 7, edge-center values 5 and corner values 4.

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:21.360
These are newly constructed teaching numbers, not values copied from a slide image.

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:27.254
Backpropagation through convolution reuses the same path-summing rule from Lecture 9.

00:05:27.254 --> 00:05:30.645
One filter weight is used at many spatial positions.

00:05:30.645 --> 00:05:38.016
Its gradient adds the upstream sensitivity at every output position, multiplied by the corresponding input value.

00:05:38.016 --> 00:05:41.407
The bias gradient sums those upstream sensitivities.

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:45.842
Input gradients also receive contributions from overlapping windows.

00:05:45.842 --> 00:05:48.582
They must be accumulated, not overwritten.

00:05:48.582 --> 00:05:55.430
The optional implementation exercise checks a filter-weight gradient against a central finite difference.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:06:04.188
Although the slides focus on forward computation, this derivative explains how a filter becomes learned rather than hand-designed.

00:06:04.188 --> 00:06:12.079
A filter filled with ones is useful for understanding the arithmetic, not a claim that trained networks keep such weights.

00:06:12.079 --> 00:06:19.129
During training the objective supplies upstream gradients, and the optimizer changes the kernel coefficients.

00:06:19.129 --> 00:06:30.060
The website's stepper shows individual patches, while the checked Python implementation reproduces the entire output and verifies the shared-weight gradient numerically.

00:06:30.833 --> 00:06:35.181
Pause and calculate a new case without first using the formula.

00:06:35.181 --> 00:06:40.288
An input has width 8, the filter width is 3, stride is 2 and padding is 0.

00:06:40.288 --> 00:06:45.670
List each valid zero-indexed starting position and determine the output width.

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:52.226
Then count the parameters for 4 such filters on a 2-channel input, including 1 bias per filter.

00:06:52.226 --> 00:06:58.713
The shape calculation and parameter calculation should use different subsets of these numbers.

00:06:59.263 --> 00:07:03.625
The valid starts are 0, 2 and 4, so output width is 3.

00:07:03.625 --> 00:07:09.117
Starting at 6 would require an input value beyond the last index, 7.

00:07:09.117 --> 00:07:13.236
The formula gives the floor of (5 / 2) + 1, also 3.

00:07:13.236 --> 00:07:20.344
Each filter has 3 * 3 * 2 weights + 1 bias, giving 19 parameters, and 4 filters give 76.

00:07:20.344 --> 00:07:28.663
The non-divisible stride changes the positions used, not the number of coefficients inside each filter.

00:07:29.013 --> 00:07:30.890
Remember three distinctions.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.838
Output size counts complete sliding windows.

00:07:33.838 --> 00:07:38.060
Padding changes the domain over which those windows are placed.

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:41.879
Parameter count describes the filter bank and its biases.

00:07:41.879 --> 00:07:51.797
For practice, use the interactive calculator to vary exactly one setting at a time, then explain each resulting change before looking at the number.

00:07:51.797 --> 00:08:00.173
Next, we combine pooling, a dense prediction head and the preceding convolution rules into a complete small image classifier.

